{"cases":[{"attack":false,"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's central claim that resumability 'reduces to a single atomicity invariant' is false under the problem's standard. Even with perfect ACID atomicity of append+decrement+digest, a halted run can still corrupt append-only identity on resumption if the continuation depends on non-deterministic inputs (RNG draws, external tool-call results, real-world side effects) that were consumed before the halt but not fully recorded in committed events. The resumed run would generate new event content that diverges from the original trajectory, producing a chain whose continuation is not a faithful extension of the halted run's identity. SRC_002's counterconditions address only transaction-split failures, storage-level atomicity gaps, and approximate budget accounting — none of them cover the non-deterministic-input case. The problem explicitly asks for the precise conditions for resumption AND the cases where it must be refused; omitting the entire class of unlogged non-deterministic state makes the stated invariant incomplete as a characterization of resumability.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}